From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Zurker <91011862+hershate@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 05:24:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Papo: batch 150 (R10) bounded residency for structure
 templates - decouple pieces, sweep the manager cache. The 0.87.0 census
 attributed the largest remaining unbounded non-content cluster to structure
 templates: 495,771 StructureTemplate$StructureBlockInfo (11.9MB) + their
 ~495k BlockPos (11.9MB) + list arrays ~= 26-28MB (3.4%) held by just 412
 StructureTemplate instances cached forever in the single server-wide
 StructureTemplateManager.structureRepository unbounded ConcurrentHashMap
 (vanilla never evicts; onResourceManagerReload only clears on datapack
 reload). The pinning audit found the hazard that made a plain bound
 insufficient: TemplateStructurePiece held a strong 'protected
 StructureTemplate template' field acquired via getOrCreate, and pieces live
 inside StructureStarts held by RESIDENT CHUNKS - so manager eviction alone
 would free almost nothing while generated chunks stay loaded. Fix is
 two-sided. (1) Pieces no longer hold template instances:
 TemplateStructurePiece keeps the manager + resolved Identifier and its
 template() accessor lazily re-fetches via getOrCreate; the field was never
 serialized (vanilla already persists the template NAME), so the disk format
 is untouched; the five direct field uses in
 ShipwreckPieces/OceanRuinPieces/NetherFossilPieces (all placement-time
 getSize/getBoundingBox) now go through the accessor; postProcess fetches once
 per pass so a single placement sees one instance. Templates are immutable
 while being placed, so reload-on-miss reproduces identical content. (2)
 structureRepository's runtime type is now PapoStructureCache (declared field
 type stays Map - compiled plugin references keep their field descriptor):
 idle eviction (120s TTL, sweep driven from MinecraftServer.tickServer every
 512 ticks because worldgen never touches the cache again after generation
 finishes), protection for every entry that entered through put() (vanilla
 getOrCreate calls get() first - the tryLoad path - and only puts when NO
 source can load the id, so the protected set is exactly the possibly-unsaved
 programmatic work: getOrCreate-created empties and every Bukkit
 CraftStructureManager register/load-with-register), access stamping on every
 read path (get, containsKey, computeIfAbsent, entrySet/values iteration) so
 plugins polling the raw map - Bukkit getStructure/getStructures - keep
 entries hot and their API semantics are byte-identical, and a 256-entry cap
 on the evictable population that evicts oldest-stamped first for pathological
 worlds. tryLoad-backed entries stay evictable because they are always
 reloadable; Optional.empty negatives (unloadable ids) are evictable and their
 reload just re-attempts the sources. Reload (datapack change) clears
 everything including protection, matching vanilla clear() semantics.
 Coverage: PapoStructureTemplateCacheTest 10 tests over an injected clock -
 idle TTL eviction, put-protection surviving a 10,000s sweep, access-stamp
 heat windows, clear-resets-protection, entrySet/get stamping (the Bukkit
 parity contract), cap eviction ordering (300 evictable -> 256 + protected,
 oldest first), reload-after-eviction transparency, exact Map contract (same
 Optional instance through get/put/computeIfAbsent/remove), back-to-back sweep
 idempotence.


